    "result_text": "All 263 tests pass, including the new `AcceptanceTest`. The build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI replaced the direct level rule with a points system. Membership is now scored as points, and the level is derived from the points.\n\n**Points formula** (start at 0):\n- `+2` when an email is present\n- `+1` when `namesakeCount == 0`\n- `+2` when the owner is in a household of 3 or more members\n- `+3` when tenure (days since registration) exceeds 365\n\n**Level mapping:** 1 (0\u20131 pts), 2 (2\u20133), 3 (4\u20135), 4 (6+). Both `membershipPoints` and `membershipLevel` are returned.\n\n**Changes:**\n- `src/main/resources/openapi.yml` \u2014 added the read-only `membershipPoints` field and rewrote `membershipLevel`'s description/bounds (now 1\u20134).\n- `mapper/MembershipPoints.java` (new) \u2014 pure helper with `points(owner, householdSize)` and `level(points)`.\n- `mapper/OwnerMapper.java` \u2014 dropped the old inline `membershipLevel` expression; both fields are now populated by the controller.\n- `rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java` \u2014 populates both fields on `getOwner`, `addOwner`, and `listOwners`, sizing each owner's household from the owner set.\n- `rest/controller/v2/OwnerRestControllerV2.java` \u2014 populates both fields on the paged listing so behavior isn't regressed there.\n\n**A note on the household factor:** the \"household of 3 or more\" term is the one factor that isn't a pure function of a single `Owner` \u2014 a household is the set of owners sharing a `householdId` (derived from last name + postcode). The old computation lived entirely in the per-owner MapStruct mapper, which has no way to count sibling owners, so I moved the membership computation into the controllers, where the repository is available to size households. The provided `AcceptanceTest` (fresh owner: email + namesake 0, single-member household, zero tenure \u2192 3 points \u2192 level 2) passes, and no other tests regressed.\n\nIf you'd instead intended \"household of 3 or more\" to mean something derivable from the owner alone (e.g., number of pets), let me know and I'll adjust \u2014 but I read it as the codebase's existing `householdId` household concept.",
